WEBVTT

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:08.310
 We knew that the farm provided sanctuary to people. We knew that that was a real value.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:22.840
 It's wonderful to step from the street onto the farm into that bustle of activity and people working together so harmoniously and towards a common goal, but each person with their different roles.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:29.440
 We work really hard to be a strength-based organization, and I hate using that term "barriers to employment."

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.320
 So people who are homeless, you know, do face a lot of barriers to employment.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.820
 There's a lack of stability because people are addressing basic survival needs.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:44.340
 Where am I going to sleep tonight? How am I going to get food? Where can I use a bathroom? How can I take a shower?

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:51.880
 And there's a real catch-22 that people get involved in when they want to get a job so that they can get a house, but they need a house to get a job.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:01:02.800
 That was the biggest education for me was seeing the courage of people we were working with, and the persistence, and the showing up every day, you know, against so many odds.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:13.880
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:20.360
 This season we're talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:34.120
 These people have shaped the food movement in California. We talk with a diverse group of California's rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms, kitchens, and communities that reshape the way we think about food.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:43.680
 This show is made by Devin Sampson and Chelsea Wills. Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca Murillo for making this season possible.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:56.990
 Terry Ganshorn is the executive director of the Homeless Garden Project, a three-acre organic farm and garden in Santa Cruz, California, where, as the mission statement reads, people find the tools they need to build a home in the world.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:01.410
 The project provides job training, transitional employment, and support services.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:07.770
 Trainees and volunteers grow and harvest fruits and vegetables that sustain daily lunches and fundraising farm dinners.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:17.010
 In addition, the Homeless Garden Project provides fresh produce for low-income families and nonprofits, from the AIDS Project to a program working with foster youth.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:23.190
 Derry came to the Homeless Garden Project in 1991 when she started working one-on-one with Project Trainees.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:28.990
 In this episode, Derry speaks with me about the farm as a sanctuary and job training site.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:35.410
 We also have an interview with Kathleen, a trainee at the Homeless Garden Project, as a companion episode to this interview.

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:39.590
 Find it in your podcast feed now or on the Delicious Revolution website.

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:45.500
 Why don't we start by you telling me about how Homeless Garden Project started?

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:54.080
 So the Homeless Garden Project started in 1990 by Paul Lee and Lynn Bashor, who is now Lynn Cooper.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:01.220
 And Paul had been really instrumental in starting the first homeless shelter in Santa Cruz.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:08.810
 He had done that in partnership with some other ministers who were part of the Citizens Committee for the Homeless.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:14.030
 And he and some of those ministers would spend the night in that shelter.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:20.270
 And he told me a story that they would wake up in the morning and the energy would be so raw.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:23.330
 He said it was like waking up in the streets of New York.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:34.420
 And he wondered, how could people ever dream of something better or set goals for changing their life when they were surrounded by this lack of safety?

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:39.310
 And by this ugliness of concrete and being on the street.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:47.610
 And so he had a vision to start something like a place of beauty and safety and sanctuary.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.630
 And he was a very avid and passionate gardener.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:05.960
 And he had been involved in starting projects up at the university and worked with Alan Chadwick in what was later to become the Center for Agroecology and Sustainable Food Systems.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:13.470
 And he was also really inspired by Jerry Brown and the California Conservation Corps.

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:29.540
 And he was inspired that the idea of people serving the environment and the dignity of that and discipline of work would be something valuable for the people that he was seeing at the shelter where he was staying.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:34.150
 So he had this idea of like a, he called it a homeless garden project.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:42.160
 And somebody, a friend of his had a nursery south of here in Santa Cruz and was going out of business.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.740
 And he called Paul and said, I have, I'm going out of business.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:49.440
 Do you want truckloads of herb plants?

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:57.240
 And Paul thought to himself, this is my homeless garden project and took one truckload.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:07.200
 And the next step was that he met Lynn Bashor and asked her if she would be the director on a volunteer basis.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.340
 And she agreed.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:11.550
 And she volunteered for the first nine months.

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:18.050
 And they would go down to the homeless shelter and pick up people who wanted to help build this garden.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:26.120
 They had identified a plot that was a community garden spot run by City of Santa Cruz Parks and Recreation.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:34.970
 And they started talking to the people who were building the garden about what direction to take the farm.

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:44.220
 And, you know, what mattered to them and what the people who were working on the farm said was that what they most needed was a job.

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:47.530
 And so that was how the project began.

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:51.540
 And the direction it took was towards job training and jobs.

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:54.730
 Is the garden still in the same site?

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:56.000
 No, it's not.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:08.100
 Yeah, that was Pelton Avenue right across from Lighthouse Field, which is right near the lighthouse and steamer lane, kind of a very high profile part of Santa Cruz County.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:13.360
 It was owned by City of Santa Cruz and the city did give us a lease.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:17.420
 But they told us, you know, this is a temporary plot.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:18.540
 You can't stay here.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:22.630
 We ended up staying there from 1990 till 1998.

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:29.490
 Well, so Paul started this garden and the people who were building this garden with him were volunteers from the shelter.

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:33.800
 And then how did this grow into a job training program?

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:40.470
 So I came after the first year, so I don't know all the details of the ins and outs of that first year.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:54.680
 So the project got involved with a consultant who said that all of the initial programs that Lynn had put together were really strong and viable, but that we needed to serve more people.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:59.950
 So the project looked into having new sites.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:10.690
 By that time, we had already started a CSA program and had a number of people coming every week during the harvest season to pick up CSA shares.

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:18.050
 We were actually the first CSA in our county and we decided to start two new farm sites.

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:19.930
 That must have been around 1994.

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:23.670
 One was at Natural Bridges Farm, which is where we're farming now.

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:28.680
 And one was down near Depot Park, which is where our current office is.

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:34.370
 And we decided to start providing employment for people during the rainy season.

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:45.470
 And that spawned the whole Women's Organic Flower Enterprise activities, which is creating wreaths and candles and other gift products that were sold during the holidays.

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:57.540
 And we had a garden just for the Women's Organic Flower Enterprise and just for the CSA to try to get a strong grasp on what did it take to run the CSA?

00:07:57.760 --> 00:07:59.840
 And what did it take to run the WOLF program?

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:03.360
 So I want to hear about how all the programs work together.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:09.730
 But I think first I want to hear about you and how how did you start doing this work?

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:10.810
 What brought you to it?

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:22.810
 So I had my background was in biology and I had done some research and published a paper with my professor and sort of thought that that was the direction I was going,

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:28.060
 that I was going to study science, get a Ph.D. and do research and perhaps teach.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:33.940
 And when I had my first child, I realized I didn't want to continue to do that anymore.

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:43.030
 I felt a really strong desire to give back to the community and to do something with helping people meet basic needs.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:52.180
 And while I was raising my children, I did some reading and found out about this man who really inspired me, Miles Horton.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:09:00.820
 And he started the Highlander Folk School, which was all about creating social change through the grassroots.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:12.880
 And his idea was that a person who is not actually experiencing a social problem, that their most effective role is to bring people together

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:16.900
 and help them find their own solutions to the problem that they're experiencing.

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:24.300
 And so I was I was looking for a way to be around those principles.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:32.920
 And I was doing an internship at the Resource Center for Nonviolence here in Santa Cruz, and I found Homeless Garden Project.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:36.720
 And it was very early in its first year.

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:39.620
 And I thought this is exactly what I want to do.

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:44.030
 This exactly embodies all these principles that I want to work with.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:48.650
 And I went out there and totally believed in what I saw.

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:51.910
 And it affirmed everything that I thought I would see there.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:10:02.190
 And I realized that it was a thriving, pretty complete activity in itself, but that nobody really knew about it.

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:09.740
 So I thought, well, why don't I do a newsletter and, you know, use the voices of people who are working on the farm

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:17.060
 and to tell their own stories of their own homelessness and how the farm is helping them to re-engage with the community

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:24.500
 and become a member of the community and not feel stigmatized by their homelessness and not feel excluded.

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:29.130
 So we had at that time, which we still have, we had a weekly circle meeting.

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:35.190
 And I went to circle meeting with my invitation to join me in writing this newsletter.

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:41.790
 And I was pretty sure that everybody was going to barrage me with their interest in writing something for the newsletter.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:49.350
 And there was only one person, Bill Tracy, an amazing veteran, Vietnam War veteran.

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:52.980
 And he and I worked together very closely.

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:57.710
 He wrote a very beautiful piece called A Day in the Life of the Homeless Garden Project.

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:02.010
 He was an incredible writer, an incredibly smart man.

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:05.440
 And he had the same birthday as me, so we had a lot to share.

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:10.060
 He was working through a lot of things when he was at Homeless Garden Project.

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:19.840
 He eventually left Homeless Garden Project, found a housing situation, and sort of had his own landscaping business

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:22.940
 and did really well for a period of time.

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:27.950
 He has since died, and I was able to be there right before he died and see him.

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:29.650
 We had a really deep connection.

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:32.130
 He's a very important person to me.

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:42.050
 But for the other voices at the farm, I was able to interview a lot of people and then transcribe their interviews

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:49.710
 and include a lot more voices in the initial newsletter that we did that was published in the comic news.

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:54.230
 And there was nobody at that time working one-on-one with the trainees.

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:58.370
 And so the project eventually hired me to continue doing that work.

00:11:58.370 --> 00:12:00.900
 What was it like to hear all these stories?

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:06.040
 Well, since I was a little girl, I had wondered about the contradictions.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:12.900
 You know, I attended Catholic school and was taught, treat others the way that you would want to be treated.

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:16.200
 And, you know, it was a very well-to-do school.

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.580
 And then we drove through the ghetto to get there.

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:25.980
 And I had always wondered about that contradiction and felt that there was some real hypocrisy there.

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:31.040
 And I'd also gone to school in Berkeley, and there was a lot of homelessness in Berkeley.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:37.700
 And I felt a desire to do something about it, but I really didn't have anything, any way to do it.

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:43.600
 So actually meeting people who were homeless and hearing their stories really humanized them for me.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:49.680
 And it was a huge education for me about the challenges that people were facing.

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:55.320
 And it also made me aware of my own stereotyping, even though I wanted to do something about it.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:13:02.530
 My own stereotyping about why people were homeless, and, you know, why don't they just do something about it?

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:06.290
 And what could my role be in helping people?

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:13.490
 And the capacity that people have to make change in their own lives, which is truly inspiring.

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:21.860
 Well, before we started recording, we were talking a little bit about how Homeless Garden Project has shifted their goals

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:25.710
 from an overall goal of well-being to job training.

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:34.480
 Yeah, so in the early days of the project, you know, the project was sort of finding what its goals were

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:40.630
 and finding how to speak about the changes that were happening for people on the farm.

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:46.840
 And at the same time, the project had goals for our enterprises and goals for our farm.

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:50.000
 And those all three kind of moved forward together.

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:53.380
 We knew that the farm provided sanctuary to people.

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:56.130
 We knew that that was a real value.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:05.680
 One of the professors that we work with up at UCSC, Heather Bullock, and her student, who is now a PhD herself,

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:10.750
 published a paper about stigmatization and social exclusion.

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:18.630
 And, you know, for some of us who don't live with exclusion or stigmatization or stereotyping,

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:24.590
 it might be hard to really understand how deeply important having a space away from that is.

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:28.030
 But their paper is really nice at describing it.

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:29.690
 So maybe I'll give you the link to that.

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:32.550
 We knew that people were increasing in their well-being.

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:39.890
 And our model at the beginning was that people would define well-being in ways most meaningful to them.

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:43.630
 So for one person, it might mean becoming sober.

00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:47.270
 And for another person, it might mean getting into housing.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:50.770
 So we were having all of the trainees set goals.

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:54.790
 And then if they met the goal, we felt that we had been successful.

00:14:55.910 --> 00:15:01.810
 And Heather also helped us to develop a matrix that we use to measure well-being.

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:11.400
 And so we would use that matrix when people first enter the project and when they left to see what kinds of changes had happened during their time here.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:16.020
 But we were also all along trying to help people get jobs.

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:23.850
 And I was able, on behalf of the Homeless Grime Project, to participate with the National Transitional Jobs Network

00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:29.280
 in an initiative they started a few years ago, must have been around 2007,

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:33.760
 called Working to End Homelessness, National Community of Practice.

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:45.170
 And they gathered together 22 organizations from all over the country who used transitional jobs as a way to end homelessness for their participants.

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:48.340
 And we did phone calls every month.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:51.240
 And there was always a theme, a learning theme.

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:53.020
 And then it ended in a conference.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:58.300
 It was a really rich experience for me to talk to other people who were doing a similar thing

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:04.510
 and to learn from their experiences and to sort of take myself outside of the project

00:16:04.510 --> 00:16:08.200
 and look onto it from a different perspective.

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:15.060
 And when I came back from that conference, I started talking to our staff and our board

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:21.480
 about changing our goals from just being well-being to the people we get jobs.

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:28.720
 And my thought was that we had always had that goal, but we hadn't explicitly articulated it

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:30.900
 and that there was a real power to doing that.

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:34.520
 So we articulated that as a goal.

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:46.620
 And then every year since, our program has developed in alignment with that goal to find structures and methods to support people to get employment.

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:49.530
 It changed our hiring practices.

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:51.350
 It changed our outreach practices.

00:16:52.450 --> 00:17:01.860
 We started a social work program that's considered a best practice for hiring people with the term that's used is barriers to employment.

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:05.460
 It's such a contradiction in this field.

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:09.140
 We work really hard to be a strength-based organization.

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:12.300
 And I hate using that term, barriers to employment.

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:17.230
 So people who are homeless, you know, do face a lot of barriers to employment.

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:20.140
 There's a lot of time spent on basic survival.

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:25.090
 There's a lack of stability because people are addressing basic survival needs.

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:26.310
 Where am I going to sleep tonight?

00:17:26.580 --> 00:17:27.980
 How am I going to get food?

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:30.370
 Where can I use a bathroom?

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:31.530
 How can I take a shower?

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:34.160
 You know, where can I get mail sent?

00:17:34.500 --> 00:17:37.490
 How can I get to a computer and write a resume?

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:41.300
 Sometimes people have legal issues that they need to resolve.

00:17:42.110 --> 00:17:50.190
 And there's a real catch-22 that people get involved in when they want to get a job so that they can get a house, but they need a house to get a job.

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:56.430
 So it is a best practice to offer support services to help people set goals.

00:17:56.430 --> 00:18:09.140
 You know, for what is their path for getting into housing, getting some of their basic needs met, and using our program for one year so that they can get a job in permanent housing.

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:13.460
 And the social workers don't act as experts on trainees' lives.

00:18:13.740 --> 00:18:15.220
 The trainee is the expert.

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:29.360
 So there's a sense of empowerment just in that very process of providing an ally who is supporting the trainee to find their own path, and then supporting the trainee in meeting their own goals that they set.

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:34.960
 So we developed that after we switched our goal from just well-being to getting a job.

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:41.680
 And we went from a three-year program to a two-year program, and then finally to a one-year program.

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:54.050
 And the reason why we did that is what we learned is that transitional jobs programs are on average about six months long, and the span is from three months to nine months.

00:18:54.860 --> 00:19:02.120
 And we wanted to find a way to serve more people, and we realized if our program length was shorter, we could serve more people.

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:13.600
 And we also noticed that people fall in love with the farm, and it's hard to leave, and it didn't seem like those next two years were helping people to learn more skills

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:25.240
 so that they could get a job, and that it seemed that people could accomplish that job goal in a year, and actually make that transition more easily than if they sort of hung on for three years.

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:32.730
 Can you talk about the work that people actually do at Homeless Garden Project, and what is job training?

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:36.350
 Like, how is a garden a place that job training happens?

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:37.430
 What goes on?

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:37.870
 Right.

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:44.010
 So our work is really seasonal, but I'll focus for this discussion here on the farming season.

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:52.850
 So we have 17 positions, and we have a farm manager and a production lead and training and education supervisor,

00:19:52.850 --> 00:19:58.030
 and they all work together with our 17 trainees and a whole bunch of volunteers.

00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:09.960
 We also have a volunteer coordinator to do all of the farming work involved in operating our CSA in our farm stand and in growing the raw materials that we use in our value-added enterprise.

00:20:11.110 --> 00:20:13.750
 The trainees work Tuesday through Friday, 9 to 2.

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:21.040
 There are occasionally opportunities for extra work, and Tuesdays we start off with a circle meeting,

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:30.350
 which is where everybody, who I just mentioned, sits in a circle, and our training and education supervisor will pose a question.

00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:38.310
 It's usually a question that has some relationship to well-being, and it might be something like,

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:45.110
 how do you deal with challenges, or what are some goals that you're inspired by right now?

00:20:45.350 --> 00:20:49.330
 And then we go around in a circle, and everybody will answer that question.

00:20:49.330 --> 00:20:57.120
 And it's a nice way to reconnect with each other, and it's an opportunity for people to get to know each other,

00:20:57.160 --> 00:21:00.460
 even if they may not work closely that day or that week.

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:02.950
 It's an opportunity to do public speaking.

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:06.470
 It's followed up by some announcements.

00:21:07.190 --> 00:21:15.450
 We have a social worker who comes and reads job openings that are—a social worker checks for job openings every week

00:21:15.450 --> 00:21:24.620
 and then reads out maybe six to ten openings so that the conversation at the farm is very much about, you know,

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:30.030
 the larger community and the idea that this is a transitional jobs program,

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:35.980
 and the goal is for people to get into jobs, and there are jobs in the community that are available.

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:44.160
 And someone, you know, even somebody who's just started for the first month and is still settling into their program,

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:47.660
 they can think about, oh, would I want that job?

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:51.100
 Or, oh, that job actually sounds like something I might want to do.

00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:53.700
 So that's Tuesday mornings, the first hour.

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:57.520
 We always have stretch every morning before we start work,

00:21:58.310 --> 00:22:05.810
 and our farm manager and the training supervisor and the production lead talk to our trainees about what the day's priorities are.

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:08.330
 There's a chalkboard when we're doing harvests,

00:22:08.530 --> 00:22:15.010
 and each person will take one of the items that needs to be harvested and cross it off and then go harvest.

00:22:15.010 --> 00:22:21.380
 We have divided all of the farm tasks into areas so that the trainees have posts,

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:24.330
 and they're in their posts for two months.

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:29.870
 There's a kitchen post, and those people work with volunteers to cook lunch.

00:22:30.670 --> 00:22:36.670
 We serve a lunch every day, Tuesday through Friday at noon, using produce grown from the farm.

00:22:36.830 --> 00:22:44.030
 And we sometimes serve up to 50 people at those lunches, including our trainees and staff and volunteers.

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:53.150
 There's a farm stand post, and that person greets volunteers and works with customers and answers questions of any kind.

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:58.820
 We have some value-added products there at the farm stand, so they might be selling them.

00:22:58.820 --> 00:23:05.690
 There might be a family coming out to harvest strawberries, so they'll direct them to how to do that,

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:10.390
 and then take the customer's money and use all the proper cash handling procedures.

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:12.770
 There's a greenhouse crew.

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:13.980
 That's my favorite.

00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:20.430
 That crew will work to start all the seedlings that are on the sowing list for that week,

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:25.500
 and the sowing list is based off our crop plan that our farm manager puts together.

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:33.440
 Not all farms start their own seedlings, but we do, and it's a really great training skill to have,

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:38.020
 and it's very detail-oriented, and it includes a lot of record-keeping.

00:23:38.580 --> 00:23:42.680
 There's a vegetable production crew, and there's many people on that crew.

00:23:43.140 --> 00:23:51.000
 It includes people doing irrigation, building beds, tending the beds, harvesting, doing pest management,

00:23:51.400 --> 00:23:57.120
 and then there's a flower production crew, and that crew is similar to the vegetable production crew,

00:23:57.120 --> 00:24:05.110
 but they're focusing on the flowers, including the flowers that we're harvesting to dry for our value-added enterprise.

00:24:05.770 --> 00:24:13.180
 It's wonderful to, from the street onto the farm, into that bustle of activity,

00:24:13.800 --> 00:24:21.690
 and people working together so harmoniously and towards a common goal, but each person with their different roles.

00:24:22.030 --> 00:24:24.730
 And then the volunteers are integrated into that as well.

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:29.890
 Last year, we had 2,500 people who volunteered at our project during the year,

00:24:30.280 --> 00:24:33.180
 and, you know, sometimes it's a big, large group coming.

00:24:33.580 --> 00:24:40.030
 Sometimes it's an intern who's doing a six-month internship with us and is working 32 hours a week.

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:45.830
 Sometimes it's a volunteer who comes one time and maybe was visiting from out of town and doesn't come again.

00:24:45.830 --> 00:24:49.710
 Can you tell me about the different ways that community members can participate?

00:24:50.110 --> 00:24:54.450
 Yeah. One of the main ways community members participate is through volunteering.

00:24:54.450 --> 00:25:03.590
 We also have community members who are CSA members and come every week during the 23-week harvest season and pick up their shares.

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:10.150
 We have two kinds of shares, the you-pick share and the shares that we harvest ourselves.

00:25:10.910 --> 00:25:14.630
 And our harvest season runs from the end of May through the end of October.

00:25:15.200 --> 00:25:17.380
 CSA members bring their families.

00:25:17.740 --> 00:25:20.720
 They bring visitors from out of town to the farm.

00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:26.980
 They're really deeply invested in what we do, and we're so grateful for their partnership.

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:30.820
 So another way is to be a customer at our downtown store.

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:34.860
 That's currently open every Friday, Saturday, and Sunday.

00:25:35.260 --> 00:25:41.400
 And then in November, we'll begin to be open seven days a week, 10 hours a day through Christmas time.

00:25:42.420 --> 00:25:45.840
 People can buy from our online store as well.

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:48.620
 And then, of course, people can be donors.

00:25:48.620 --> 00:25:57.740
 We have various fundraising campaigns, and more than 65% of our income comes from donations from individuals.

00:25:58.280 --> 00:26:02.910
 One of the programs that we have that people can donate to is called Feed to Birds.

00:26:03.310 --> 00:26:12.140
 And in that program, donors help to set aside shares that we distribute to agencies that work with low-income people.

00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:21.870
 This year, we're working with 10 different agencies, and we hope to distribute 5,000 pounds of food to them during the 23 weeks that they'll partner with us.

00:26:21.870 --> 00:26:29.350
 And the agencies range from the Boys and Girls Club of Santa Cruz County, the Housing and Urban Development Veteran Administration,

00:26:29.550 --> 00:26:31.430
 and Supportive Housing Program.

00:26:31.430 --> 00:26:34.730
 So those are the two newest programs that we're working with.

00:26:34.810 --> 00:26:44.900
 So we're distributing food grown by our trainees to low-income children in our community and to veterans who are just getting into housing.

00:26:45.160 --> 00:26:49.400
 We also support the River Street Shelter that way and the Santa Cruz AIDS Project.

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:54.940
 We work with a program that engages foster youth that are transitioning out of foster care.

00:26:54.940 --> 00:26:57.560
 We have a really sweet partnership with them.

00:26:57.920 --> 00:27:00.640
 The youth that they serve are so inspiring.

00:27:00.820 --> 00:27:06.800
 They come to some of our events, and there's a photo show of some of them using and cooking the food that we grow.

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:10.520
 We did a health fair with these agencies this year.

00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:12.100
 It was called a food and wellness fair.

00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:14.930
 They all came and talked about how they use the food.

00:27:15.570 --> 00:27:24.750
 We had a demonstration about how to grow vegetables at your own home in a container so that people could increase their consumption of fresh fruits and vegetables.

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:29.890
 The Dominican Hospital Mobile Health Van came out and did health screenings.

00:27:30.640 --> 00:27:39.420
 And three agencies did cooking demonstrations about how to cook organic produce in tasty ways that are affordable.

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:53.190
 And Leanne Brown, who wrote this fantastic cookbook called Good and Cheap, How to Eat Well on $4 a Day, she donated books to us, so we were able to give those books out to the people who participated that day.

00:27:53.190 --> 00:28:02.250
 We have a dinner series called the Sustained Dinner Series, and chefs from the community come, and each chef cooks one of four courses.

00:28:02.710 --> 00:28:07.520
 Our most recent dinner, we had 160 guests, and we always have a speaker.

00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:19.340
 This last dinner, it was the secretary of the California Department of Food and Agriculture, and she came and spoke about the importance of local agriculture in our economy.

00:28:20.060 --> 00:28:30.090
 And then one of our trainees spoke at that event as well about her experience falling into homelessness and how the project has served her.

00:28:31.480 --> 00:28:35.090
 Those are always the most inspiring and fun parts of the event for me.

00:28:35.090 --> 00:28:39.290
 Well, thank you for being on the show today, and we're going to do something a little different.

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:48.250
 And with this episode, we're also going to interview someone who is a trainee at the Homeless Garden Project and hear their story.

00:28:48.510 --> 00:28:53.070
 So I'm really looking forward to that, and we'll get a whole nother perspective.

00:28:53.510 --> 00:28:53.970
 Thank you.

00:28:54.520 --> 00:28:57.040
 I can say one more way that people can be involved.

00:28:57.220 --> 00:28:57.500
 Great.

00:28:57.500 --> 00:29:11.490
 We're really looking for employers who want to join our Employer Ally Network and who are interested in learning more about who our trainees are and what kinds of jobs they're looking for and might have jobs to offer to our trainees.

00:29:11.830 --> 00:29:12.210
 Wonderful.

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:18.070
 And is there any other way that we should know how to follow along with what's going on with Homeless Garden Project?

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:20.080
 Yes, we have two websites.

00:29:20.260 --> 00:29:33.100
 We have our regular HomelessGardenProject.org website, and then we also have one called Store.HomelessGardenProject.org that talks about how to visit our downtown store.

00:29:33.100 --> 00:29:38.640
 It has a link to our online store, and it also talks about all of the events that we're doing.

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:51.970
 And then finally, we have a Facebook page, and there's a lot of ways to get a feeling for the day-to-day of what's happening on the farm and to just celebrate the farm and the people that we're serving.

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:52.390
 Perfect.

00:29:52.670 --> 00:29:56.470
 Well, I'll make sure to include those in the notes with the episode.

00:29:56.730 --> 00:29:58.230
 So thank you so much.

00:29:58.920 --> 00:29:59.360
 Absolutely.

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:00.280
 Thank you, Chelsea.

